<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abraha was an Aksumite general who seized power in Himyar, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, in the 530s and ruled it, nominally as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, until about 570. He built a great church at Sanaa and repaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a> dam, recording his deeds in inscriptions. Islamic tradition credits him with an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> with elephants, remembered in the Qur'an's Surah al-Fil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraha.jpg" alt="Abraha" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abu Ali Bal'ami · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balami_-_Tarikhnama_-_The_miraculous_rescue_of_the_Ka%27ba_from_Abraha%27s_attack_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
